// Interviewer asks: "How would you implement rate limiting in a REST API?"
// You haven't built this before, but you can reason through it:

@RestController
public class OrderController {
    private final Map<String, List<Long>> requestTimestamps = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

    @PostMapping("/orders")
    public ResponseEntity<?> createOrder(@RequestBody OrderRequest request,
                                          HttpServletRequest httpRequest) {
        var clientId = httpRequest.getRemoteAddr();

        // Remove timestamps older than 1 minute
        var now = System.currentTimeMillis();
        var recentRequests = requestTimestamps
            .getOrDefault(clientId, List.of())
            .stream()
            .filter(timestamp -> now - timestamp < 60_000)
            .collect(Collectors.toList());

        if (recentRequests.size() >= 100) {
            return ResponseEntity.status(429).body("Rate limit exceeded");
        }

        recentRequests.add(now);
        requestTimestamps.put(clientId, recentRequests);

        // Process the order
        return ResponseEntity.ok("Order created");
    }
}

// Question: "What are the limitations of using @Transactional?"

@Service
public class OrderService {
    @Autowired
    private OrderRepository orderRepo;

    @Autowired
    private EmailService emailService;

    @Transactional
    public void processOrder(Order order) {
        orderRepo.save(order);

        // LIMITATION 1: Transaction holds DB connection during slow I/O
        emailService.sendConfirmation(order); // This might take 2 seconds

        // LIMITATION 2: Doesn't propagate to new threads
        CompletableFuture.runAsync(() -> {
            orderRepo.updateStatus(order.getId(), "SHIPPED"); // No transaction here!
        });

        // LIMITATION 3: Only rolls back on RuntimeException by default
        try {
            externalApi.notify(order);
        } catch (IOException e) {
            // Checked exception - transaction won't rollback!
            throw new RuntimeException(e); // Must wrap it
        }
    }
}

// Interviewer asks: "Have you worked with message queues like RabbitMQ?"
// You haven't, but you've built REST APIs. Bridge the concepts:

@RestController
public class OrderController {
    @Autowired
    private PaymentService paymentService;

    // Current synchronous approach you know
    @PostMapping("/orders")
    public ResponseEntity<String> createOrder(@RequestBody Order order) {
        var result = paymentService.processPayment(order); // Blocks here
        if (result.isSuccess()) {
            return ResponseEntity.ok("Order created");
        }
        return ResponseEntity.status(500).body("Payment failed");
    }
}

// You explain: "I haven't used RabbitMQ, but I understand the problem it solves.
// In my current code, the payment call blocks the request thread.
// If payment takes 5 seconds, the user waits. With a message queue, I'd
// publish the order to a queue, return immediately, and let a separate
// worker process the payment asynchronously. This decouples the services
// and improves response time. I'd start learning it by setting up a
// simple producer-consumer example and comparing it to my REST approach."

// Interviewer asks: "How does Java handle memory management?"
// You start explaining manual memory management, then realize they meant GC:

// Your initial wrong direction (manual memory):
public class MemoryExample {
    public void allocateMemory() {
        var buffer = ByteBuffer.allocateDirect(1024); // Direct memory
        // You're explaining: "Java lets you allocate off-heap memory..."
    }
}

// You pause mid-explanation and course-correct:
// "Actually, wait—are you asking about garbage collection specifically,
// or manual memory management? Let me focus on GC since that's Java's
// primary approach."

public class GarbageCollectionExample {
    public void demonstrateGC() {
        var orders = new ArrayList<Order>();
        for (int i = 0; i < 1000; i++) {
            orders.add(new Order(i, "Item " + i));
        }
        // Once orders goes out of scope, GC can reclaim this memory
        // Developers don't call free() or delete like in C++
    }

    // GC automatically reclaims objects with no reachable references
    // using mark-and-sweep or generational collection algorithms
}
